WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:08.630
Speaker 1: We want to hopefully be done with
this question of is the former President Donald

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:14.809
Trump going to be able to hide records, phone
records and other records from the lead up

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:22.089
to and the immediate aftermath of the January
20, January six, 2021, Trump riots?

00:00:22.089 --> 00:00:30.060
The Supreme Court is now rejecting Donald
Trump's bid to hide the riot records.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.599
Is this finally going to be the end?

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:33.840
I really hope so.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.590
First of all, remember the context?

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:41.450
This is about riot records being provided
to the January 6th House Committee that is

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:42.800
investigating the Trump riots.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.670
ABC News, CBS News rather reports.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:50.970
The Supreme Court on Tuesday turned away former
President Donald Trump's legal fight with

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:56.100
the White House, with the House Select Committee
investigating the Jan. six assault on the

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.640
U.S. Capitol bringing an end.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.970
We can only hope bringing an end to the former
president's quest to stop investigators from

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:09.409
accessing reams of documents from his final
weeks in the White House.

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:14.780
Trump asked the Supreme Court in December
to take up his appeal of a decision from the

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:20.359
federal appeals court in Washington, D.C.,
which rejected his efforts to shield the documents

00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:26.420
from lawmakers and found Trump provided, quote
no basis for it to override President Biden's

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:32.229
decision not to uphold his predecessor's claim
of executive privilege over the records.

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:37.289
In rejecting the former president's request,
the decision from the U.S. Court of Appeals

00:01:37.289 --> 00:01:41.389
for the District of Columbia Circuit remains
in place.

00:01:41.389 --> 00:01:46.139
In addition to asking the Supreme Court to
review the lower court's decision, Trump requested

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:52.799
the justices stop the transfer of records
from the National Archives to House investigators.

00:01:52.799 --> 00:01:57.200
They declined to do so last month, and the
select committee received more than 700 pages

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.420
of records sought by the panel earlier this
month.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.959
So is this finally the end?

00:02:02.959 --> 00:02:06.149
I really hope so because I am so sick of this.

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:07.889
He was president.

00:02:07.889 --> 00:02:09.800
Those are our records.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.400
These are not Trump's documents to control.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.020
These are our records, and they should go
to the committee investigating this.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.350
And of course, you are innocent until proven
guilty.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:21.810
Trump has not been charged with anything.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.900
He gets an opportunity for all of that stuff.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.200
In a colloquial setting like this, which is
not a court of law, this is just a show.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:37.450
One would naturally wonder why would you be
so anxious for those records not to become

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:38.450
public?

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:43.910
If you have nothing to hide now, there are
two explanations, and I want to be fair.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.780
One explanation is he has something to hide.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:52.070
That's a completely reasonable colloquial
explanation we might all come to without saying

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.230
that means he is legally guilty of anything.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:55.850
He just he has stuff to hide.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:56.850
Fine.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:03.920
The other one, and it could be this is that
it's a really effective legal strategy.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.730
You always push back and you always obstruct
everything.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:15.070
Even really simple requests, uncontroversial
requests, even if later it'll be revealed

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.670
there was no criminal wrongdoing there or
whatever.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:25.120
And that makes it harder to figure out when
is Trump really being sketchy and really has

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.120
something to hide?

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:32.490
What I mean by that is imagine that there
were ten requests made of Donald Trump and

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.980
Trump's really only concerned about one of
those requests, and that's the only one that

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:37.980
he fights.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:42.400
You're you're telegraphing where investigators
should really be paying attention.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.830
You're signaling where is the wrongdoing really
hidden?

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:50.780
If there are ten requests made and you're
only worried about one of them, but you try

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:57.480
to obstruct all 10, you are making it more
difficult to determine which of these 10 things

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.400
should we really be focusing on.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:04.100
And so I think that that it's completely plausible
that that's just the legal strategy here.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:09.580
And that's why Donald Trump is doing this
in terms of Trump's willingness to put records

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:10.930
out there.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:17.430
Remember that he was just about to publish
those tax returns back in 2015 and then 2016

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.410
as soon as the IRS audit was done.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.750
And we never got those tax tax records.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.000
So we know he never has any intention of publishing
anything.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.530
Last thought, imagine being an adult.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:35.740
And seeing Trump's behavior forget about during
his presidency, but just over the last year

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.150
since he's been out of office and still being
impressed with him and thinking that he's

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:44.500
an alpha and thinking that he's smart and
a good businessperson and a good decision

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:51.090
maker, it says almost more about the people
that still support the guy than it does about

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.790
Trump himself.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:53.790
Send me your thoughts.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:59.340
You can find us on Facebook at facebook.com
The David Pakman Show.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.291
After this break, we're going to hear from
Merrick Garland and much more.

